Subject: [PATCH] DRM: apply shift state when producing charPressedQueue
 entries

PollKeyboardEvents() had a pre-existing, upstream-acknowledged
TODO/FIXME: evkeyToUnicodeLUT[event.code] is a static, unshifted-only
lookup table, so charPressedQueue (what GuiTextBox()/GetCharPressed()
actually consume for text input) never produced uppercase letters or
shifted symbols regardless of Shift key state - confirmed always
reproducible (not a hotplug/timing issue at all, despite initially
looking like one, per real hardware testing) and confirmed to affect
both the disk-backed and initramfs image variants equally, since both
share this same raylib build.

Adds a standard US-QWERTY shift transform, applied only to the
charPressedQueue entry (raw keycodes/currentKeyState, used for
non-text-input purposes like keyboard shortcuts, are untouched -
Shift as a modifier for those continues to work via
IsKeyDown(KEY_LEFT_SHIFT)/(KEY_RIGHT_SHIFT) exactly as before, this
patch only affects what character text fields receive).

Verified: the shift-transform function itself tested in isolation
against all 21 US-QWERTY digit/punctuation pairs plus letter
case-folding plus already-uppercase/unmapped/space pass-through (27
cases total, all passing). Full file compiles cleanly with this
change applied on top of the existing multi-keyboard-fd patch.

Upstream-Status: Pending

diff --git a/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c b/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
index 2380194e..8a0ac678 100644
--- a/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
+++ b/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
@@ -193,6 +193,46 @@ static const int evkeyToUnicodeLUT[] = {
     // LUT currently incomplete, only mapped the most essential keys
 };
 
+// PATCH: evkeyToUnicodeLUT above is unshifted-only (pre-existing
+// upstream TODO/FIXME on the line that uses it) - this applies a
+// standard US-QWERTY shift transform on top of it, so text fields
+// (GuiTextBox()/GetCharPressed()) can actually produce uppercase
+// letters and shifted symbols. Letters: simple case-fold. Digit row
+// and punctuation: explicit unshifted->shifted pairs, standard US
+// layout. Anything not in this table (already-uppercase-range
+// values, control characters, unmapped/0 entries) is returned
+// unchanged - deliberately conservative rather than guessing.
+static int ApplyShiftToUnicodeChar(int ch)
+{
+    if (ch >= 'a' && ch <= 'z') return ch - 32;
+    switch (ch)
+    {
+        case '1': return '!';
+        case '2': return '@';
+        case '3': return '#';
+        case '4': return '$';
+        case '5': return '%';
+        case '6': return '^';
+        case '7': return '&';
+        case '8': return '*';
+        case '9': return '(';
+        case '0': return ')';
+        case '-': return '_';
+        case '=': return '+';
+        case '[': return '{';
+        case ']': return '}';
+        case ';': return ':';
+        case '\'': return '"';
+        case '`': return '~';
+        case '\\': return '|';
+        case ',': return '<';
+        case '.': return '>';
+        case '/': return '?';
+        default: return ch;
+    }
+}
+
+
 // This is the map used to map any keycode returned from linux to a raylib code from 'raylib.h'
 // NOTE: Use short here to save a little memory
 static const short linuxToRaylibMap[KEYMAP_SIZE] = {
@@ -2239,8 +2279,13 @@ static void PollKeyboardEvents(void)
 
                     if (CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueueCount < MAX_CHAR_PRESSED_QUEUE)
                     {
-                        // TODO/FIXME: This is not actually converting to unicode properly because it's not taking things like shift into account
-                        CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueue[CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueueCount] = evkeyToUnicodeLUT[event.code];
+                        // PATCH: shift now applied via ApplyShiftToUnicodeChar() above -
+                        // checks raylib's own, already-updated-by-this-function keyboard
+                        // state, not a separate/duplicate tracking mechanism.
+                        int unicodeCh = evkeyToUnicodeLUT[event.code];
+                        if (CORE.Input.Keyboard.currentKeyState[KEY_LEFT_SHIFT] || CORE.Input.Keyboard.currentKeyState[KEY_RIGHT_SHIFT])
+                            unicodeCh = ApplyShiftToUnicodeChar(unicodeCh);
+                        CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueue[CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueueCount] = unicodeCh;
                         CORE.Input.Keyboard.charPressedQueueCount++;
                     }
                 }
